We can rewrite the expression as
\begin{align*}
\sum_{n = 2}^{10000} \frac{1}{n \sqrt{n - 1} + (n - 1) \sqrt{n}} &= \sum_{n = 2}^{10000} \frac{1}{\sqrt{n(n - 1)}} \\
&= \sum_{n = 2}^{10000} \frac{1}{\sqrt{n} \sqrt{n - 1}} \\
&= \sum_{n = 2}^{10000} \left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{n - 1}} - \frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}\right).
\end{align*}
The sum telescopes, so most of the terms cancel out:
\[\sum_{n = 2}^{10000} \left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{n - 1}} - \frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}\right) = \left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{1}} - \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\right) + \left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} - \frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}\right) + \dotsb + \left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{9999}} - \frac{1}{\sqrt{10000}}\right).\]
The terms in the middle cancel out, leaving us with
\[\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{1}} - \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\right) - \frac{1}{\sqrt{10000}} = 1 - \frac{1}{100} = \boxed{\frac{99}{100}}.\]The answer is: \frac{99}{100}

